# Mesh #48f243e4 — semgrep baseline triage for the upcoming GitLab CI gate.
#
# .github/workflows/** and the rest of .github/ belong to the GitHub showcase
# mirror of this repo (see CLAUDE-task-workflow.md §0z) — canon CI for evc-mesh
# is .gitlab-ci.yml on GitLab, and .github/ is not what runs against this repo's
# real pipeline. The semgrep job being added here gates GitLab CI, so scanning
# the mirror's own workflow files is out of scope for it.
#
# This is NOT a silent dismissal of what semgrep finds there: 87 real findings
# (mostly github-actions-mutable-action-tag: workflows pinning a third-party
# Action by a mutable tag like `@v4` instead of a commit SHA, plus a handful of
# run-shell-injection findings from untrusted `${{ }}` interpolation directly
# into a `run:` shell step) are a genuine pre-existing supply-chain issue on
# the mirror. They are tracked as their own follow-up card rather than folded
# into this baseline.
.github/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A repo-level .semgrepignore REPLACES semgrep's built-in defaults — it does not
# add to them. Measured on this tree (`semgrep scan --x-ls`, target-file count):
#
#   with this file, before the block below   24609 targets, 21716 in node_modules
#   with this file removed entirely           2548 targets,     0 in node_modules
#
# So the moment any job scans a checkout that has dependencies installed, an
# ignore file written as "just the one extra path we want excluded" silently
# turns the whole vendor tree into scan surface — minutes of runtime and
# findings in code we do not own. `:include .gitignore` pulls our own ignore
# rules back in; the literals below cover what .gitignore does not.
:include .gitignore

node_modules/
.git/
dist/
build/
coverage/
vendor/
*.min.js
*.min.css
